CHANGELOG

4.3.0 (in development):

- The bundle is the product, and there is no persistent database
  (ADR-003). A build creates a throwaway SQLite, uses it, and leaves a
  parquet bundle behind. PostgreSQL still works for building; nothing
  reads from it.
  - Two measurements drove it. `variant_molecular_effects` costs about
    255 bytes per row in a row store and 4 in parquet, which puts the
    full genome at roughly 426 GB against 6.65 GB. Everything else — the
    entire entity model — is about 7 million rows and 105 MB, and was
    being hosted on a server sized for the 2 billion variant rows beside
    it.
  - Alembic is gone. There is no migration chain: a database is built
    once by `db create-db` with `create_all`, and a schema change
    produces a new bundle rather than an in-place upgrade.

- New command group `bundle`: `plan`, `build`, `info`
  - `bundle plan` writes the recipe — every data source with an include
    flag, its DTP and version, split into the two branches. List order
    is the dependency declaration, since the core branch resolves
    entities against what ran before it.
  - `bundle build` runs both branches and assembles only once every
    planned source has succeeded. A bundle missing a table looks exactly
    like a complete one to whoever reads it, so nothing is published
    unless everything worked.
  - The core branch stages through SQLite because those sources resolve
    entities against each other. The variant branch writes its final
    parquet directly and has no load step at all — `load()` raises by
    design.
  - Resume is the default: an interrupted build re-runs only what is
    pending, because the staging ledger records both branches even
    though only one stores data there. `--restart` discards it.
  - Disk, not CPU, is the binding constraint. Raw files are dropped as
    soon as their parquet exists; `--keep-raw` disables that, and
    `--min-free-gb` (default 100) stops a source starting into a disk
    that cannot hold it.
  - `--into` folds a run's variant output into an existing bundle, so a
    genome can be built in stages. The bundle id changes each time
    because it is derived from content; `build_record.json` records the
    id before and after every merge. Re-folding a chromosome already
    present is refused rather than silently doubling its rows.
  - `--no-assemble` and `--keep-processed` support the same staged
    workflow.
  - `bundle info` prints what a bundle says about itself. `created_at`
    is when the bundle began and `updated_at` when a merge last changed
    it; neither reaches the fingerprint, so the id still identifies
    content rather than a run.

- The gnomAD DTP reads the joint callset, so a bundle now carries three
  data sets where it carried one
  - The superseded DTP read `gnomad.genomes.v4.1.sites.chr*.vcf.bgz` —
    genomes only. The current one reads
    `gnomad.joint.v4.1.sites.chr*.vcf.bgz`, and the 20 INFO fields it
    keeps hold exomes, genomes and the joint aggregate side by side: AC,
    AF, AN and nhomalt for each, plus the joint filtering allele
    frequencies, the group-max figures, and both callsets' filter flags.
  - Coverage grows accordingly. A variant observed only in the exome
    callset is in the bundle now; before it was simply absent, and
    nothing said so.

- A parameterised frequency filter on the variant branch, applied at
  build time
  - `dtps/config/dtp_variant_gnomad_joint.json` carries `min_ac`,
    `max_ac`, `min_af` and `max_af`, against `AC_joint` and `AF_joint`.
    `null` disables a bound and bounds are inclusive. The default is
    `min_ac: 5`, which produces a bundle of roughly 25 GB.
  - Measured on chromosome 21: `AC_joint = 0` accounts for 49.5% of
    records, singletons for a further 22.2%, and `AC > 5` keeps 14.3%.
    So the default is already most of the size decision, and the two
    directions cost differently — lowering the bound to reach rare
    variants is expensive, raising it is how a smaller bundle is built.
  - Set it before the build. Bundles are immutable (ADR-003), so a
    variant dropped here is absent from that bundle for good: the filter
    decides what the artifact will contain, and is not a query-time
    option a reader can relax later.

- The report layer was rewritten to read parquet natively (ADR-004).
  The old module is deleted, not frozen.
  - A bundle is opened, not connected to. `Bundle.open()` reads the
    manifest, verifies every declared file is present at its declared
    size, starts an in-process DuckDB and registers one view per logical
    table. No Engine, no sessionmaker, no Session, no ORM.
  - Views come from the manifest rather than a directory scan. Inferring
    table membership from filenames is what let an empty parent file
    shadow 177 million rows — the same defect, independently, in two
    code paths.
  - Each execution gets its own cursor, so temp tables and registered
    relations no longer leak between reports sharing a process.
  - Input arrives as a registered relation and is joined, never
    interpolated. That is what keeps injection out and what turns a
    ten-thousand-value filter into a hash join.
  - A report declares `requires` (checked before the query runs, so a
    missing source says so in one line) and `optional` (absent tables
    are recorded, because a column null for want of a source looks
    exactly like a null answer).

- Reports renamed and consolidated. 17 reports in six families:
  `resolve_*`, `annotate_*`, `expand_*`, `pair_*`, `aggregate_*`,
  `platform_*`. The old names are gone; each new report's explain guide
  names its predecessor.
  - entity_filter                          -> resolve_entity
  - annotation_master_gene                 -> annotate_gene
  - annotation_master_variant              -> annotate_variant
  - annotation_master_{disease,go,
    pathway,protein}                       -> annotate_*
  - entity_relationship_model              -> expand_entity_relationship
  - entity_neighborhood_summary            -> expand_entity_neighborhood
  - gene_to_variant_filtering,
    variant_annotation_expanded            -> expand_gene_to_variant
  - annotation_variant_regulatory_evidence -> expand_variant_regulatory
  - variant_gene_location_model,
    variant_single_gene_annotation,
    snp_snp_model, variant_modeling,
    snp_snp_pair_generator                 -> pair_variants
  - variant_list_intersect,
    variant_binning                        -> aggregate_cohort_variants
  - etl_status                             -> platform_etl_status
  - etl_packages                           -> platform_etl_packages
  - annotation_master_chemical, db_pg_table_stats and db_pg_index_stats
    are retired with no replacement.
  - New: `pair_genes` — gene pairs linked by shared biology, and the
    item pairs those gene pairs imply for a mapping the caller supplies
    (ADR-005).

- What a report returns is now a documented object, not a DataFrame
  - `ReportResult` carries the main table, any further tables, the
    provenance, and files the report wrote.
  - Provenance records the report, the bundle id and root, the
    parameters, the row count, the coverage gaps, and `warnings` — which
    is always present, so an empty list means nothing went wrong rather
    than nobody having checked.
  - `emit(name, table)` attaches a second table when an answer is
    genuinely two shapes. `platform_data_statistics` returns `storage`
    and `variants` beside its long list; `aggregate_cohort_variants`
    returns the (variant, bin) mapping beside the bins.
  - `save(dir)` / `ReportResult.load(dir)` round-trip the whole thing,
    losing nothing, in a bundle's own layout — so `Bundle.open` reads a
    saved result and DuckDB queries it. `load()` reports whether the
    source bundle is still on disk.
  - `write(path)` remains the export: one table, nested columns
    flattened to JSON so a spreadsheet can hold them, lossy on purpose.
    CSV gets a `.provenance.json` sidecar; parquet carries provenance in
    its own metadata as well.
  - Parameter and input values are case-insensitive everywhere, across
    14 places in 8 reports. Previously true by consistent habit; now
    asserted by a test.

- Pointing at data is a path, not a URI
  - `--bundle <dir>` on the CLI, `BIOFILTER_BUNDLE` in the environment,
    `[database] bundle` in `.biofilter.toml` (resolved relative to that
    file), and `Biofilter(bundle=...)` in Python.
  - Point at the bundle root, the directory holding `manifest.json`, not
    at its `tables/` subdirectory.
  - Passing `--bundle` and `--db-uri` together is an error rather than a
    guess about which was meant.
  - `--db-uri` remains for what is genuinely a database: a staging
    SQLite during a build, or a development PostgreSQL.

- Version checking between a bundle and the install that reads it
  - A bundle declaring an unsupported `manifest_version` is refused,
    with a message saying to update Biofilter rather than migrate the
    bundle.
  - A bundle built by a different release opens and warns, recording the
    mismatch in `provenance["version_mismatch"]`. Advisory on purpose:
    refusing would make an archived bundle unreadable by the only
    install at hand, and a bundle cannot be rebuilt once its sources
    have moved on.
  - Opening a bundle warns about columns this build expects and does not
    find; `db verify --in <bundle> --schema` makes the same check an
    error, for gating.

- Schema
  - Seven variant tables that were declared and never written are
    deleted: variant_biotypes, variant_consequence_categories,
    variant_consequence_groups, variant_effect_predictions,
    variant_gene_regulatory_evidence, variant_gwas_snp,
    variant_regulatory_elements. They shipped as empty tables, which is
    worse than absent — `db verify` counted them present and a report
    joining one got zero rows instead of an error.
  - variant_consequences and variant_impacts stay, keyed by name rather
    than a surrogate id. They carry `severity_rank`, which the
    annotation does not and which cannot be derived from it.
  - The variant tables are keyed by chromosome, position, reference and
    alternate allele. They carry no entity id and no foreign keys, which
    is what lets the variant branch be built without the entity graph
    existing yet.

- Performance, all of it query shape rather than layout
  - `aggregate_cohort_variants` resolved rsIDs by a join whose condition
    mixed an equality with `IS NULL`, which stops DuckDB using a hash
    join: the plan was a nested loop over 264 million rows, and it ran
    for every cohort including the VCFs that already carry coordinates.
  - The same join called `lower()` on an rsID column that is already
    entirely lowercase. A plain hash join finds three rsIDs among 264
    million in 1.22 s; the report took 68.93 s.
  - `annotate_gene` measured all 39,306 placed genes to answer about
    one: 63.6 s for one gene and the same 63.6 s for ten. A cost that
    does not move with the question is a scan that ignores it.
  - Four reports queried partitioned variant tables without naming the
    chromosome, so every file in the bundle was read. Invisible while a
    bundle held one chromosome; a minute per report once it held six.

- Containers: one image, two mounts
  - The two images merged. `docker/hpc/` is gone; the same image serves
    Docker and Apptainer, published to Docker Hub and to GHCR under the
    `biofilter-hpc` name existing pull commands already use.
  - The contract is `/bundle` read-only and `/workspace` writable, with
    `BIOFILTER_BUNDLE` defaulting to `/bundle`, so a normal run names no
    paths beyond the binds.
  - Fix: the entrypoint appended `--db-uri "$DATABASE_URL"` whenever
    that variable was set, and since `--bundle` and `--db-uri` together
    is a usage error, a leftover DATABASE_URL made the normal 4.3
    invocation fail citing an argument the caller never passed.
  - Fix: `docker run <image> report list` tried to exec `report`, the
    `biofilter` prefix having been added only when the first argument
    began with a dash.

- Documentation reorganized by audience: the root of `docs/source` and
  `getting_started/` for someone reading a bundle, `technical/` for
  whoever builds one or extends Biofilter. Every page was rewritten
  against the code. `parquet_backend.md` became `technical/read_path.md`
  — "backend" implied a pluggability that no longer exists.
  - New Penn LPC guides at `notebooks/lpc__quickstart.md` and
    `lpc__deploy.md`, replacing the 4.2.x pair now frozen under
    `biofilter_legacy/`.
  - The GPT assistant kit is rescoped to bundle readers, with the
    context manifest as the single source of truth for its knowledge
    base — both sync paths read it.

- Breaking changes
  - Every report renamed; see the table above. `report list` is the
    authority, and each explain guide names its predecessor.
  - Reports no longer run against PostgreSQL or SQLite. They read a
    bundle.
  - `bf.report.run()` returns a `ReportResult`, not a DataFrame. Call
    `.to_pandas()`.
  - `pair_variants` takes variants and nothing else. Gene input, the
    gene-to-variant expansion, `max_variants_per_gene` and `membership`
    are gone (ADR-005 D13). The gene path hid a choice belonging to the
    caller: a gene on chr22 holds about 4,000 variants and the report
    kept 100 of them ranked by frequency, with nobody seeing which. Run
    `expand_gene_to_variant` first and the list is in front of the
    person filtering it.
  - `db migrate` and the Alembic chain are gone.
  - The dbSNP DTP and a superseded gnomAD DTP were removed.

4.2.1 (29/07/2026):

- Parquet bundle foundation — turns the bundle into a first-class build
  artifact rather than a one-off export
  - `parquet://` now registers hive-partitioned datasets: a `<table>/`
    directory becomes one VIEW over `<table>/**/*.parquet`, with the
    partition key returned as a real column (e.g.
    `variant_masters/chromosome=1/part-0.parquet`)
  - One directory per chromosome preserves file-level pruning on
    `WHERE chromosome = N` and lets a single chromosome be rebuilt
    without rewriting the rest
  - Flat `<table>.parquet` files still work; legacy `_chr_` sibling
    files are dropped only when their parent is present, since an
    orphan child is the only copy of its data
  - `exists_db()` now recurses, so a bundle holding only partitioned
    datasets is recognised
- Manifest v2 and bundle verification
  - Manifest gains per-file size and SHA-256, the payload format,
    whether partition children were included, and ETL provenance
    (source system, data source, version tag, package counts) — a
    bundle can now report which DTP versions built it without access to
    the originating database
  - New command `biofilter db verify --in <bundle>` validates a bundle
    against its manifest with no database connection and exits 1 on
    problems, so CI can gate on it; `--no-hashes` checks presence and
    size only
  - New `db export` flags: `--include-partition-children` and
    `--no-checksums`
- Fix: JSON columns broke every report that selected them on the
  Parquet backend
  - psycopg2 returns JSON already decoded and SQLAlchemy's PostgreSQL
    dialect disables its deserializer accordingly; duckdb_engine also
    returns a dict, but the generic dialect still applied the
    deserializer, so `json.loads()` was called on a dict
  - `etl_status` raised; `etl_packages` swallowed the error in a bare
    except and returned 0 rows instead of 55 — a silent wrong answer
  - A tolerant deserializer is now installed on the DuckDB engine only;
    PostgreSQL keeps the dialect's own handling
- Fix: export duplicated all partitioned variant data
  - `get_table_names()` returns both partitioned parents and their
    children (43 + 125 on the dev database), while `SELECT *` on the
    parent already returns every child's rows, so bundles stored each
    variant row twice — and view registration then skipped the
    duplicated half entirely
  - Since import replays whatever the manifest lists, this also
    double-inserted on round-trip
  - Children are now excluded by default; detection filters on
    `relkind`, because partitioned indexes also carry `relispartition`
- Correction to the 4.2.0 notes: read-only is NOT enforced. A
  `parquet://` connection is an in-memory DuckDB in which only the
  parquet-backed VIEWs are immutable — `CREATE TEMP TABLE` works, and
  several reports depend on it. `Database.read_only` was never read
  anywhere in the codebase; it is now documented as advisory.
- Validation harness (`scripts/spikes/bundle_readonly/`): every report
  run against PostgreSQL and against a Parquet bundle built from the dev
  database. 24 of 26 reach parity; the 2 that do not are PostgreSQL-only
  by explicit design (`db_pg_index_stats`, `db_pg_table_stats`). 5
  reports require external input files and remain unexercised. The
  harness doubles as a regression test for this release.
- New DTP `kegg_relationships` (20 active DTPs), with its explain guide
  and registration in the data-source and ETL-process seeds; `dtp_kegg`
  refactored alongside it
- New helper `compute_files_hash()` for data sources made of several
  downloaded files that need a single hash covering all of them, so the
  ETL skip logic still applies
- Documentation catches up with the move off the VPS: production is the
  read-only Parquet bundle on the Penn LPC; the former PostgreSQL
  deployment procedure is kept in `lpc__deploy.md` Appendix A;
  `.biofilter.toml` drops the VPS connection strings, which carried
  credentials and a public IP
- No models, DDL, migrations or reports changed

4.2.0 (22/06/2026):

- New backend: read-only DuckDB over a Parquet bundle
  - New URI scheme `parquet:///path/to/bundle/tables` recognised by the
    Database class
  - Translates internally to `duckdb:///:memory:` plus auto-registration
    of one SQL VIEW per *.parquet file in the directory (skipping
    `_chr_*` partition children — the consolidated parents are queried)
  - Uses SQLAlchemy StaticPool so every session shares the in-memory
    catalog where the views live
  - DuckDB's natural rejection of writes against `read_parquet` views
    enforces read-only at the storage layer; the new `Database.read_only`
    flag exposes the same information at the application layer
- New dependencies: `duckdb`, `duckdb-engine` (read-only Parquet path)
- Existing PostgreSQL and SQLite backends are unchanged — the new mode
  is purely additive
- POC validation (logged in notebooks/Andre/ADR/0001-...):
  - 10,000 rsIDs annotated through `variant_molecular_effects`
    (1.79 billion rows) in 1.18s with peak memory 89 MB on local NVMe
  - Same workload on the LPC over GPFS: 15.52s — still well below any
    practical threshold
  - End-to-end CLI report run (`annotation_master_variant` for three
    rsIDs) completes in 1.22s
- Operational impact: HPC deployments no longer require a bundled
  PostgreSQL container, a 480 GB pgdata bind mount, or per-user copies.
  The bundle is shared, read-only, multi-user-safe.
- Documentation: ADR-001 (`notebooks/Andre/ADR/0001-duckdb-parquet-
  strategy.md`) describes the rationale, alternatives, and the SQLite
  migration attempts that motivated this change.

4.1.4 (02/06/2026):

- Extended variant_modeling output with 6 new columns
  - variant_1_input / variant_2_input — original input string(s) the user supplied (comma-joined when multiple inputs resolve to the same variant_id); usable as join key to merge results back into the caller's source table
  - variant_1_ref / variant_2_ref — reference allele from variant_masters
  - variant_1_alt / variant_2_alt — alternate allele from variant_masters
- variant_modeling lookup queries now select reference_allele and alternate_allele alongside chromosome and position
- Updated report_variant_modeling.md with new column descriptions and a "Merging results back to the caller's table" example using variant_*_input

4.1.3 (01/06/2026):

- Added chr:pos:ref:alt input format to annotation_master_variant and variant_modeling reports
  - Returns the exact ref/alt variant when alleles are specified (SNV or indel)
  - Falls back to all-alleles-at-position behavior when only chr:pos is given (existing format unchanged)
  - REF/ALT are case-insensitive (uppercased on lookup); base validation [ACGT]+
  - Resolves multiallelic ambiguity for credible-set / fine-mapping inputs
- Expanded annotation_master_variant explain doc
  - Detailed descriptions of most_severe_only and canonical_only (when to use, fallback behavior, combination)
  - Note clarifying eQTL/sQTL data is not covered here; cross-reference to annotation_variant_regulatory_evidence
- Added "Running on the UPenn LPC (Apptainer)" section to both report notebooks
  - Heredoc pattern for input file preparation
  - --params-file pattern for list parameters (avoids shell double-quote stripping inside the container)
  - Cross-links to lpc__quickstart.md and lpc__deploy.md
- Synced biofilter/utils/version.py with pyproject.toml (both now 4.1.3)

